course: dictionary
lesson: 7029
title: Partial, not whole
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Partial means not complete. It means only part of something. In «A partial water change and a filter help», the goldfish tank gets some fresh water, but not all of it. If you can say "only part of it" and it still makes sense, partial fits.
explain: Partial can also mean unfair. A referee who is partial favours one team, so the other team does not get a fair game. The opposite is impartial, which means fair to everyone. The im- at the front works like "not", so impartial means "not partial".
explain: In everyday talk, "I'm partial to a scone" means I like scones a lot. This is informal, and you will hear it from grown-ups and in polite or older speech. Check the words around the word: does the sentence mean only part, unfair, or liking something?
fact: Partial means not complete, or only part of something.
fact: Partial can also mean unfairly supporting one side or person more than another.
fact: Impartial means fair, not taking sides. It is the opposite of partial in the unfair sense.
fact: A «partial eclipse» is when only part of the Sun or Moon is covered.
fact: To be «partial to» something means you like it a lot. This is informal.
exercise: «A partial water change and a filter help» a goldfish that is gulping at the surface. What does partial water change mean? | *Changing only some of the water in the tank | Feeding the fish twice a day | Wiping the glass with a cloth | Changing all the water in the tank
exercise: A football referee who always backs one team is partial. What does partial mean here? | Liking football a lot | *Unfairly supporting one side | Very quick at running | Only part of the game is finished
exercise: Ella says, «I'm partial to a scone.» What does she mean? | Ella is baking scones for the whole class | Ella has only half a scone left | Ella is unfair to scones | *Ella likes scones a lot
test: Which word means fair, not taking sides? | Complete | *Impartial | Partly | Partial
test: What does a «partial eclipse» mean? | *Only part of the Sun or Moon is covered | The whole Sun is covered | The Sun is shining very brightly | The Moon is missing
exam: Which sentence uses partial to mean only part of something? | The referee was partial to Team A | The partial cheer came from the crowd | *The eclipse was partial, so only part of the Sun was dark | I'm partial to chocolate
